FinTwin: data sources and their terms
=====================================

The MIT licence in LICENSE covers the FinTwin source code only. The data that the code downloads, and the data files
derived from it (web/public/data/, engine/fintwin/data/*.csv, the frozen research snapshot, docs/paper/research.json),
come from the sources below. This note summarises where they come from; it is not legal advice, and the terms of each
source, not this file, are authoritative. If you redistribute or build on the data, check the source's current terms.

1. SEC EDGAR (company facts, filings, 8-K Item 1.03 bankruptcy notices)
   Source: U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, https://www.sec.gov. Filings and the XBRL "company facts" API are
   public information. SEC asks automated users to identify themselves with a User-Agent and to stay under
   10 requests per second (https://www.sec.gov/os/webmaster-faq#developers); FinTwin does both. Derived company-level
   figures in this repository (calibrated parameters, simulated outputs, scores) are computed by FinTwin from those
   filings and are not SEC data. No endorsement by the SEC is implied.

2. FRED (Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis): macro series (GDP, interest rates, NBER recession indicator and others)
   Source: https://fred.stlouisfed.org. Each series has its own original source and terms (some series are third-party
   data republished by FRED, with their own copyright notices). Series are fetched at run time through the public CSV
   endpoint; the frozen snapshot and web/public/data contain only the values the model used. See the notes on each
   series page at FRED and https://fred.stlouisfed.org/legal/ before redistributing a series.

3. Philadelphia Fed Real-Time Data Set for Macroeconomists (GDP vintages as published on past dates)
   Source: Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia, https://www.philadelphiafed.org/surveys-and-data/real-time-data-research.
   Used so that nominal GDP is the value available on the forecast date, not today's revised one. Please cite the
   dataset (Croushore and Stark, 2001) and check the Bank's terms of use before redistributing the series.

4. Damodaran synthetic-rating tables (interest coverage to rating and default spread)
   Source: Aswath Damodaran, NYU Stern, https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/. The January tables of past years are
   read from copies archived by the Internet Archive (Wayback Machine). The tables are the author's work; the values
   in engine/fintwin/model/ratings.py are used as inputs with attribution, not republished as a dataset. Contact the
   author for any use beyond that.

5. Index membership lists (engine/fintwin/data/sp500_*.csv, universe_sp500.csv)
   Constituent tickers and CIKs of the S&P 500 on named dates, taken from public revisions of the Wikipedia constituents
   table (text under CC BY-SA 4.0) and checked against SEC filings. "S&P 500" is a trademark of S&P Dow Jones Indices;
   FinTwin is not affiliated with or endorsed by it.

The FinTwin code is MIT-licensed (LICENSE). The numbers FinTwin computes are the author's results, to be cited as in
CITATION.cff; the underlying source data keeps the terms of its source.
